DETAILED ACTION
Notice of Pre-AIA or AIA Status
AIA
1. The present application is being examined under the pre-AIA first to invent provisions.
Information Disclosure Statement
2. The information disclosure statement (IDS) submitted on 2/2/2016 is in compliance with the provisions of 37 CFR 1.97. Accordingly, the information disclosure statement is being considered by the examiner.
Claim Rejections - 35 USC § 102
3. The following is a quotation of the appropriate paragraphs of pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102 that form the basis for the rejections under this section made in this Office action:
A person shall be entitled to a patent unless –
(a) the invention was known or used by others in this country, or patented or described in a printed publication in this or a foreign country, before the invention thereof by the applicant for a patent.
4. Claims 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 11, 12, 14, 16, 17, 19 and 20 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102a as being anticipated by Lee et al. (US Publication Number 2007/0271424, hereinafter “Lee”).
5. As per claims 1, 11 and 20, Lee teaches a method and memory control component of controlling a memory module having memory components disposed thereon (figure 11), the method comprising: outputting first and second memory read commands to the memory module at respective times (multiple read commands are outputted to the memory module based at timing, paragraphs 85 and 86); receiving first read data concurrently (concurrent reception of data in memory for command operation) from a first plurality of the memory components via a first plurality of data paths, respectively, in response to the first memory read command (commands associated with a first memory m1, paragraph 86); and receiving second read data concurrently from a second plurality of the memory components via a second plurality of data paths, respectively, in response to the second memory read command (commands associated with the second memory m2, paragraph 86), the first plurality of the memory components including at least one memory component not included in the second plurality of the memory components and vice-versa (concurrent yet separate handling of the commands with respect to the memory where the first particularly when dealing with MG1 versus MG2 and the memory structure therein). 6. As per claims 2 and 12, Lee teaches a method and memory control component further comprising outputting a maintenance command to the at least one memory component included in the first plurality of the memory components but not included in the second plurality of the memory components, the maintenance command instructing the at least one memory component to execute a maintenance operation during an interval that transpires concurrently with receiving the second read data from the second plurality of the memory components (control signal is the maintenance command instructing the memory component to handle a particular operation concurrently with the pending memory operations, paragraph 46). 7. As per claims 4 and 14, Lee teaches a method and memory control component wherein outputting the first and second memory read commands to the memory module at respective times comprises outputting the first and second memory read commands to first and second pluralities of nonvolatile memory components (time difference in outputting, paragraph 15). 8. As per claims 6 and 16, Lee teaches a method and memory control component wherein outputting the first and second memory read commands to the memory module at respective times comprises, at a first time, outputting chip-select signals (chip selection signal line, paragraph 44) exclusively to the first plurality the components while outputting first command signals on a command signaling path coupled to each of the memory components of the first and second pluralities, and, at a second time outputting chip-select signals exclusively to the second plurality of the memory components while outputting second command signals on the command signaling path (paragraph 45, where each MG1 and MG2 have independent CS lines). 9. As per claims 7 and 17, Lee teaches a method and memory control component further comprising outputting a third memory read command to the memory module after outputting the first and second memory read commands, and receiving third read data from the third plurality of memory components via a third plurality of data paths, respectively, in response to the third memory read command, the third plurality of memory components including (i) at least one memory component not included in the first plurality of memory components and (ii) at least one other memory component not included in the second plurality of memory components (MG3, third memory component with respective data path and memory components with read concurrent mechanism in place, figure 11).
10. As per claims 9 and 19, Lee teaches a method and memory control component wherein outputting the first memory command to the memory module comprises receiving a request to read data from the memory module, obtaining a value that indicates the first plurality of memory components and outputting chip-select signals exclusively to the first plurality of the memory components (paragraph 45, where each MG1 and MG2 have independent CS lines).
Claim Rejections - 35 USC § 103
11. The following is a quotation of pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) which forms the basis for all obviousness rejections set forth in this Office action:
(a) A patent may not be obtained though the invention is not identically disclosed or described as set forth in section 102, if the differences between the subject matter sought to be patented and the prior art are such that the subject matter as a whole would have been obvious at the time the invention was made to a person having ordinary skill in the art to which said subject matter pertains. Patentability shall not be negatived by the manner in which the invention was made.
12. Claims 3, 5, 10, 13 and 15 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Lee in view of Tsern et al. (US Publication Number 2007/0088995, hereinafter "Tsern").
13. As per claims 3 and 13, Lee teaches a method and memory control component wherein outputting the first and second memory read commands to the memory module at respective times comprises outputting the first and second memory read commands (figure 11, paragraph 86), however does not explicitly disclose teachings of a dual inline memory module (DIMM).
Tsern teaches outputting the first and second memory read commands to the memory module at respective times comprises outputting the first and second memory read commands to a dual inline memory module (DIMM) (figure 9a, DIMM) at respective times via a command/address path coupled in common to all the memory components included in the first and second pluralities (paragraph 70).
It would have been obvious to one of ordinary skill in the art at the time of Applicant’s invention to modify Lee with the above teachings of Tsern. One of ordinary skill would be motivated to make such modification in order to enhance memory module efficiency (paragraph 37).
14. Lee modified by the teachings of Tsern as seen in claim 3 above, as per claims 5 and 15, Tsern teaches a method and memory control component wherein the nonvolatile memory components comprise Flash memory components (paragraph 53).
15. Lee modified by the teachings of Tsern as seen in claim 3 above, as per claim 10, Tsern teaches a method wherein obtaining the value that indicates the first plurality of memory components comprises obtaining the value from a page table based at least in part on an address received in association with the request to read data (paragraph 115).
16. Claims 8 and 18 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Lee in view of Bhakta et al. (US Publication Number 2006/0062047, hereinafter "Bhakta").
17. As per claims 8 and 18, Lee teaches a method and memory control component wherein the at least one memory component included in the first plurality of the memory components but not included in the second plurality of the memory components (figure 11), however does not explicitly disclose virtual pair functionality.
Bhakta teaches a method and memory control component wherein the at least one memory component included in the first plurality of the memory components (paragraph 50) but not included in the second plurality of the memory components comprises a first memory component of a virtual pair of components (paragraph 78, virtual pairs), and the at least one memory component included in the second plurality of the memory components but not included in the first plurality of the memory components comprises a second memory component of the virtual pair of components, the method further comprising copying data from the first memory component of the virtual pair to the second memory component of the virtual pair (paragraph 80).
It would have been obvious to one of ordinary skill in the art at the time of Applicant’s invention to modify Lee with the above teachings of Bhakta. One of ordinary skill would be motivated to make such modification in order to enhance memory capabilities in a system (paragraph 11).
